course: dictionary
lesson: 2601
title: Deport: a serious word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To deport someone means to force them to leave a country. This usually happens when they are not allowed to live there, or have broken the law. «In May 1944 Stalin's regime deported the whole Crimean Tatar people to Central Asia.» The words «the whole» and «to Central Asia» show that many people were sent far away from their homes against their will.
explain: Deport is a serious word, so look at the clues around it. In the sentence, many people «died on the way and in the first years». That tells you deportation was harsh and dangerous, not a simple move. When you read deport in news or history, ask: who is being forced to leave, and why?
explain: Deport also has an old-fashioned meaning: to behave in a certain way. You will sometimes see it in the phrase «deport yourself», which means how you act. «She will deport herself well at the party» means she will behave well. Look at the words around it to choose the right meaning.
fact: To deport means to force someone to leave a country.
fact: People may be deported if they do not have permission to stay in a country.
fact: «Deport» is a verb, and «deportation» is the noun: the act of deporting people.
fact: «Ukraine recognises it as genocide»: this shows that deportations of whole peoples can be remembered as very serious crimes.
fact: «Deport yourself» is old-fashioned and means to behave in a certain way.
exercise: «In May 1944 Stalin's regime deported the whole Crimean Tatar people to Central Asia.» What does deported mean here? | Given a holiday | *Forced to leave their homeland | Invited to a party | Taught to speak a new language
exercise: «She will deport herself well at the school dance.» What does deport yourself mean here? | Leave the room quietly | Dance very fast | *Behave in a certain way
exercise: Why might someone be deported? | They won a prize at school | They speak two languages | They have a birthday in the country | *They do not have permission to stay in the country
test: Which word is the noun form of deport? | deported | deporting | deporter | *deportation
test: Choose the best word: «Many families were ___ from their homes in 1944.» | deports | deport | *deported | deporting
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to deport when it means to force someone to leave? | forgive | dance | welcome | *expel
